Some of the equilibrium constant values are small, which means the amount of reactants decomposing into products $x$ is also small. That scenario simplifies, to a large extent, the degree of the polynomials involved in the equilibria. For example,
the equilibrium constant for the formation of ammonia at 720$^{\circ}$C is $K_c$=2.4$\times 10^{-3}$
\begin{center} \ce{H_2_{(g)} + 3H_2_{(g)} <=> 2NH_3_{(g)}    }
\end{center}
being this value small. An ICE chart for this reaction would be
\begin{center}\begin{tikzpicture}
 \node[inner sep=0pt] (tab){%
 \begin{tabular}{l | l  l l ll}
       & \ce{N_2_{(g)}}  & + &3\ce{H_2_{(g)}} &  \ce{<=>}  &2\ce{NH_3_{(g)}}  \\
  \hline
  Initial     &1M  &&1M&&0M  \\
  Change    &$-x$  &&$-3x$&&$2x$    \\
  Equilibrium  &1$-x$  &&1$-3x$&&$2x$    \\
\end{tabular}
};
\node[draw=red, inner sep=0pt, rounded corners=3pt, line width=2pt,
fit=(tab.north west) (tab.north east) (tab.south east) (tab.south west)] {};
\end{tikzpicture}\end{center}
As $K_c$ is small, we expect $x$ to be small too, and therefore we can simplify the ICE table given that
\[ 1-x\simeq 1 \;\; \text{and}\;\;   1-3x\simeq 1\]
Hence the new ICE chart would be

\begin{center}\begin{tikzpicture}
\node[inner sep=0pt] (tab){%
\begin{tabular}{l | l  l l ll}
& \ce{N_2_{(g)}}  & + &3\ce{H_2_{(g)}} &  \ce{<=>}  &2\ce{NH_3_{(g)}}  \\
\hline
Initial     &1M  &&1M&&0M  \\
Change    &$-x$  &&$-3x$&&$2x$    \\
Equilibrium  &$\sim$1  &&$\sim$1&&$2x$    \\
\end{tabular}
};
\node[draw=red, inner sep=0pt, rounded corners=3pt, line width=2pt,
fit=(tab.north west) (tab.north east) (tab.south east) (tab.south west)] {};
\end{tikzpicture}\end{center}
With this simplified ICE chart, we will obtain the polynomial and solve for the extent. Using the expression for $K_c$ and substituting the values from the ICE chart:
\[ K_c=\frac{  [\ce{NH3}]^2 }{[\ce{N2}] \cdot [\ce{H2}]^3}\simeq\frac{  (2x)^2 }{(1) \cdot (1)^3}=\frac{  4x^2 }{1}  \]
The corresponding polynomial will be
\[P(x) \simeq 4x^2-K_c=0   \]
This approach simplifies P(x) by reducing its degree and hence makes it easier to solve for $x$. The positive root would be $x\sim$0.024M, this value is similar to the exact one calculated in the example above (0.022M). We can verify our approach given the calculated $x$ value with the percent error indicated in parenthesis
\[ 1-x= 0.97 (2\%) \;\; \text{and}\;\;    1-3x=0.92(7\%)\]
These results indicate the error is less than 10\%. Similarly, we see that the approximate $x$ value (0.024M) and the exact one (0.022M) also differ by less than 10\%. In general, we can use this approach when the predicted approximate values differ by less than 10\% from the exact values.


